<DocsLayout
  title="Roder Desktop"
  eyebrow="Surfaces"
  description="Use project threads, local issues, persistent agents, browser and design panels, and execution profiles in Roder Desktop."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/chrome/", title: "Chrome browser control" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/persistent-agents/", title: "Persistent agents" }}
>
  <p>
    Roder Desktop keeps a project's conversation, terminal, browser, files, and code
    review in one window. It runs the same Roder harness as the CLI through a local
    app-server process. This guide covers desktop workflows available through
    <strong>Desktop v0.1.11</strong>, which embeds <strong>Roder v0.4.1</strong>.
    Desktop and harness versions are separate release numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a project</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Install the app from the <a href="/desktop/">desktop downloads page</a> and open a project folder.</li>
    <li>Connect an inference provider in Settings, then choose a model and supported reasoning effort.</li>
    <li>Start a thread in that project. Choose its policy mode before asking the agent to work.</li>
    <li>Use adjacent panels to inspect files, run terminal commands, preview pages, and review changed code.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Threads are saved locally and can be reopened. The sidebar groups them by project
    and keeps child-agent threads out of the top-level project list. Open the
    <strong>Overview</strong> panel to inspect thread activity and subagents instead.
    Provider credentials and inference subscriptions are separate from the desktop app;
    see <a href="/docs/providers/">providers</a> for supported backends.
  </p>

  <h2>Panels beside the conversation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Terminal:</strong> run commands in a project terminal without switching applications.</li>
    <li><strong>Files:</strong> browse the workspace and open project files.</li>
    <li><strong>Review:</strong> inspect changed files and diffs alongside the thread.</li>
    <li><strong>Browser:</strong> open a local preview or let the agent inspect and interact with a page.</li>
    <li><strong>Canvas:</strong> sketch and annotate.</li>
    <li><strong>Design:</strong> edit a structured design with layers, shapes, text, tokens, and agent assistance.</li>
    <li><strong>Extensions:</strong> open installed extension panels.</li>
    <li><strong>Overview:</strong> follow thread activity and subagents.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Design documents live in project-specific <code>~/.roder/design/*.roderdesign</code>
    files. The Design panel can inspect layout and export selected nodes when the
    connected harness advertises those methods. SVG is the design export format.
    Scoped design-agent planning does not itself launch a worker: the active agent
    still needs an available delegation tool.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose the browser deliberately</h2>
  <p>
    The built-in browser is the desktop default. Invoke <code>$browser</code> to
    choose it explicitly. Use <code>$chrome</code> to route the thread to the paired
    Chrome extension instead, including a Chrome session in which you are already
    signed in. If the extension is disconnected, Roder reports that state rather
    than silently switching browsers.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <strong>Settings &gt; Browser</strong> and choose <strong>Turn on bridge</strong>.</li>
    <li>Copy the connection URL and bearer token into the Roder Browser Bridge extension's settings.</li>
    <li>Connect the extension, then invoke <code>$chrome</code> in the thread.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The bridge listens on localhost and generates a fresh token whenever it starts.
    Regenerating the token invalidates the old one. Keep the token private and do not
    expose the bridge publicly. The extension's site permissions and control settings
    still apply. See <a href="/docs/chrome/">Chrome browser control</a> for tool
    permissions and <a href="/docs/browser-decisions/">browser decisions</a> for
    bounded Jev or OpenAI Decisions goals; those are separate browser workflows.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep long threads usable</h2>
  <p>
    The transcript groups tool history between user messages, with up to five live
    tools shown while work is running. Older content is virtualized and loaded from
    indexed history as you scroll or seek a conversation section. A short rendered
    window is not a deleted conversation: earlier turns remain in persisted history.
    History navigation and code-review loading are separate from the agent's model
    context window.
  </p>
  <p>
    In v0.1.11, a project's sidebar menu offers <strong>Archive project (keeps
    conversations)</strong>. Switch to archived projects to restore it later.
    This hides a project group without deleting its folder or conversations. It is
    separate from archiving an individual thread and from archiving an Issues project.
  </p>

  <h2>Track local projects and issues</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Issues</strong> and choose <strong>New project</strong>. Link an
    existing desktop workspace if you want to launch work from its issues. An Issues
    project is an organizational record; a workspace is the folder context where a
    thread runs. Creating one does not create the other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Create an issue, then edit its title, description, project, status, and assigned
    thread. Statuses are <code>backlog</code>, <code>todo</code>,
    <code>in_progress</code>, <code>blocked</code>, <code>done</code>, and
    <code>cancelled</code>. Filter by project, status, assignment, or text. Done and
    Cancelled groups start collapsed; matching filters reveal their rows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Save an issue, reopen it, and choose <strong>Save &amp; launch work</strong> to
    start a thread in its linked workspace. An already-assigned issue opens its
    existing thread rather than launching a duplicate. Unassign it explicitly if
    you need another thread. Saving edits precedes launch, so a launch failure does
    not discard the edits.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    Issues are local, with no GitHub or Linear synchronization. Assignment is a link,
    not a scheduler: changing status does not stop a thread, and thread completion
    does not automatically close an issue. Archive an Issues project to retain its
    history while preventing new issues and launches.
  </div>
  <p>
    Agents can use the same records through <code>projects_list</code>,
    <code>projects_read</code>, <code>projects_create</code>,
    <code>projects_update</code>, <code>projects_delete</code>, and the corresponding
    <code>issues_*</code> tools, including <code>issues_launch</code>, when advertised
    to the thread. These desktop tools are not a hosted issue service.
  </p>

  <h2>Persistent agents and execution machines</h2>
  <p>
    A <a href="/docs/persistent-agents/">persistent agent</a> keeps a mission, editable
    memory, and one durable thread across check-ins. New agents are paused; creating
    one does not enable proactive work. Run allowances and time limits bound its work
    while the desktop is running.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/cloud-machines/">Cloud Machines</a> profiles select the execution
    environment for a new thread's native coding tools. They do not upload the
    repository or move desktop panels to the cloud. A profile is fixed when the
    thread is created; changing the selector does not migrate existing work.
  </p>

  <h2>Configure Roder from chat</h2>
  <p>
    Desktop v0.1.10 added the built-in <code>$roder-config</code> skill and
    <code>desktop_config</code> tool. Ask for a supported settings change in a thread
    that advertises the tool. The agent should read current settings, change only
    the requested values, and verify the result.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Choose the inference provider, model, and reasoning effort.</li>
    <li>Set web search, indexed search, shell, default policy mode, or file-backed dynamic context.</li>
    <li>Read and enable skills, adjust their exposure, or inspect and refresh workflow integrations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is an allowlisted app-server bridge, not arbitrary desktop automation or a
    way to change every Electron preference. Do not paste credentials into tool
    arguments. Enabling executable MCP or workflow integrations requires explicit
    approval; a pasted configuration is not approval to execute it. Restart-only
    changes still require a restart. Older threads without the tool need a new chat.
  </p>

  <h2>Understand usage and cost estimates</h2>
  <p>
    The thread usage popover combines recorded thread tokens with discovered subagent
    descendants, avoiding duplicate inherited history. It offers thread and model
    breakdowns, refresh, and a <strong>Scan more history</strong> action when a bounded
    scan leaves gaps.
  </p>
  <p>
    API cost estimates use a dated bundled pricing snapshot. Missing model rates,
    incomplete analytics, cache attribution, and undiscovered descendants can make
    the total partial. Usage refreshes after completion rather than providing a live
    per-token meter. These estimates are not provider invoices, subscription usage,
    machine charges, or a hard spending cap.
    See <a href="/docs/usage-analytics/">usage analytics</a> for harness-level reporting.
  </p>

  <h2>Install updates</h2>
  <p>
    Use <strong>Check for Updates</strong> in the app. Desktop v0.1.11 also shows an
    actionable update pill beside sidebar settings when an update is available.
    Follow the offered download or installation action for your platform.
    Official release builds currently target macOS Apple silicon and Windows x64;
    macOS releases use the published update feed. See the
    <a href="/desktop/">download page</a> for current installers and platform notes.
  </p>

  <h2>How desktop integration works</h2>
  <p>
    The main process starts <code>roder app-server</code> over stdio. The renderer uses
    typed protocol methods for threads, turns, tools, providers, and approvals. The
    desktop owns native windows, terminals, web views, and drag-and-drop; the harness
    owns inference, tool policy, sessions, and events. Desktop-only tools extend
    that boundary without forking the runtime.
  </p>
  <p>
    A remote app-server controller is a different architecture from a Cloud Machines
    execution profile. In the remote-controller model, the remote node owns sessions,
    tool execution, provider auth, and artifacts. A local Cloud Machines profile
    keeps the desktop's app-server connection and routes native coding execution
    through a runner. See <a href="/docs/app-server/">app server</a> for the protocol.
  </p>

  <h2>Release references</h2>
  <p>
    Persistent agents and local issues entered the v0.1.5 release line; v0.1.9 added
    Cloud Machines, indexed history, and accounting improvements. v0.1.10 added
    chat configuration and persistent-agent creation tools. v0.1.11 added sidebar
    project archives, the update pill, and full-history virtualization.
    Reference the
    <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder-desktop/releases/tag/v0.1.11">v0.1.11 release</a>,
    <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder-desktop/blob/v0.1.11/docs/issues.md">local issues guide</a>,
    and <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder-desktop/blob/v0.1.11/docs/thread-accounting.md">accounting contract</a>.
    Unreleased desktop work is not included in this guide.
  </p>
